WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.787
Before we begin, I want you to see where this intelligence starts.

00:00:03.787 --> 00:00:05.680
Rowan, take us inside Grayhaven.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.887
Welcome to Grayhaven.

00:00:06.887 --> 00:00:07.922
This is our base.

00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:09.646
That barn holds the supplies.

00:00:09.646 --> 00:00:10.336
Two routes.

00:00:10.336 --> 00:00:11.313
Three teammates.

00:00:11.313 --> 00:00:13.268
One mission: bring everyone home.

00:00:13.268 --> 00:00:14.188
Scout observes.

00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:15.107
Rick retrieves.

00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:16.027
Negan protects.

00:00:16.027 --> 00:00:19.361
We built the world, the mission, and the training system.

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:23.040
Every run gives us another way to understand how a team learns.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.845
A mission becomes a learning problem when every decision has a consequence.

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:29.541
Observe.

00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:29.928
Act.

00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:31.322
Receive feedback.

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:32.560
Practice again.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.896
Now imagine those principles operating across AEGIS.

00:00:35.896 --> 00:00:42.441
A compound that rehearses supply movements, coordinates its teams, and adapts when conditions change.

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:45.200
That is the future we are building toward.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.795
Today, we will show you the world we built, the learning inside it, and the opportunity ahead.

00:00:50.795 --> 00:00:52.640
Welcome to our annual meeting.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.053
Athena: You have seen the world.

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:56.821
Now let us look at the intelligence inside it.

00:00:56.821 --> 00:00:59.000
Mira, what are we actually teaching?

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.792
Dr. Mira Chen: We are teaching a policy: a way to choose an action from what the agent can observe.

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:06.359
Picture Rick at the edge of the prison yard.

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:10.581
He has a position, a mission, and information about the world around him.

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:11.836
He chooses an action.

00:01:11.836 --> 00:01:12.919
The world changes.

00:01:12.919 --> 00:01:14.174
He receives feedback.

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:18.054
During training, experience like this is used to update the policy.

00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:23.987
Over many attempts, the learning process searches for decisions that produce better outcomes over time.

00:01:23.987 --> 00:01:25.812
That is reinforcement learning.

00:01:25.812 --> 00:01:27.809
The important phrase is over time.

00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:33.399
Moving quickly can look useful in the moment, but a mission may depend on waiting for a teammate.

00:01:33.399 --> 00:01:37.849
Collecting supplies can look like success, but the job includes getting home.

00:01:37.849 --> 00:01:42.698
We design the environment, the available actions, the observations, and the rewards.

00:01:42.698 --> 00:01:45.151
The agent practices within that structure.

00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:50.000
Our engineering task is to make the structure express the behavior we actually want.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.345
Athena: Rowan, take us back through that mission.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:56.920
Show us why three useful individuals still need to learn how to work as a team.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.477
Rowan Vale: Start at the prison depot.

00:01:58.477 --> 00:02:03.806
The team has two outward routes, a sheltered waystation, and a supply cache in the barn.

00:02:03.806 --> 00:02:05.782
Rick carries the retrieval role.

00:02:05.782 --> 00:02:09.016
Scout has the wider view and reports the safer route.

00:02:09.016 --> 00:02:10.872
Negan has the protective role.

00:02:10.872 --> 00:02:13.088
Those abilities create dependencies.

00:02:13.088 --> 00:02:15.363
Information has to become a decision.

00:02:15.363 --> 00:02:18.178
A decision has to become coordinated movement.

00:02:18.178 --> 00:02:23.208
The team must regroup, collect the supplies, return, and bring every survivor home.

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:27.339
This footage is our scripted demonstration of that intended mission.

00:02:27.339 --> 00:02:32.669
It lets you see the world and the behavior we are asking the learning system to develop.

00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:36.381
The value of Unity is that we can make this problem concrete.

00:02:36.381 --> 00:02:41.052
We can see positions, change conditions, repeat a run, and record the events.

00:02:41.052 --> 00:02:47.160
The environment gives an abstract learning problem a place where decisions have visible consequences.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.407
Athena: That gives us a mission we can see.

00:02:49.407 --> 00:02:53.643
Mira, give our shareholders the engineering foundation behind it.

00:02:53.643 --> 00:02:55.440
What can we build on today?

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:02.218
Dr. Mira Chen: We have a working Unity environment, three specialized role policies, and a record of what happens in each run.

00:03:02.218 --> 00:03:07.286
We verified the mission mechanics across twenty-four scripted test configurations.

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:13.148
We also produced sixteen complete scripted practice trips to demonstrate the intended behavior.

00:03:13.148 --> 00:03:17.483
Those demonstrations give the learning process examples to learn from.

00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:24.200
Each of the three role policies reached a saved checkpoint of just over five hundred thousand training steps.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.939
The training combines reinforcement learning with behavior cloning from those demonstrations.

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:36.228
These are different parts of the foundation: the mechanics, the examples, and the trained checkpoints.

00:03:36.228 --> 00:03:38.366
Our dashboard keeps them distinct.

00:03:38.366 --> 00:03:46.731
We can save a team, hold its policy fixed, repeat a defined test suite, and inspect its route reports, mission milestones, and outcomes.

00:03:46.731 --> 00:03:50.089
End-to-end reliability is the next research milestone.

00:03:50.089 --> 00:03:56.134
What we have built is the environment and the measurement system that make that progress testable.

00:03:56.134 --> 00:03:59.920
That turns the project into a repeatable engineering process.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.273
Athena: Now step forward into the AEGIS future we are imagining.

00:04:03.273 --> 00:04:05.368
Elias is at the training compound.

00:04:05.368 --> 00:04:08.960
Elias, show us what these principles could become at scale.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.615
Elias Ward: Welcome to the proving ground.

00:04:10.615 --> 00:04:14.256
In this vision, the compound is a connected learning environment.

00:04:14.256 --> 00:04:19.001
Cargo vehicles move supplies between a receiving hangar and the teams that need them.

00:04:19.001 --> 00:04:21.042
Maintenance crews inspect equipment.

00:04:21.042 --> 00:04:23.801
A control room watches the condition of the site.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:29.483
Before a change reaches the physical operation, its decision process can be rehearsed in a simulation.

00:04:29.483 --> 00:04:31.524
A vehicle encounters a blocked lane.

00:04:31.524 --> 00:04:32.793
A delivery is delayed.

00:04:32.793 --> 00:04:34.890
A piece of equipment needs attention.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:40.738
We ask the same question you saw in Grayhaven: can local decisions add up to a successful shared mission?

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:44.324
The setting is larger, but the underlying challenge is familiar.

00:04:44.324 --> 00:04:45.813
Information is incomplete.

00:04:45.813 --> 00:04:47.082
Resources are limited.

00:04:47.082 --> 00:04:48.958
Actions affect what happens next.

00:04:48.958 --> 00:04:53.923
Reinforcement learning gives us a way to explore policies for those sequential decisions.

00:04:53.923 --> 00:05:02.640
The proposed value is better preparation: more useful practice, clearer comparisons, and fewer surprises when people make decisions about the real operation.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.604
Athena: A compound cannot depend on every day looking the same.

00:05:05.604 --> 00:05:10.400
How does the training ground prepare a team for conditions it has not rehearsed exactly?

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:13.418
Elias Ward: We vary the conditions and keep the mission understandable.

00:05:13.418 --> 00:05:20.835
In the AEGIS scenario, a supply vehicle might begin from a different position, a route might take longer, or a service bay might be unavailable.

00:05:20.835 --> 00:05:24.415
We want to see how the policy responds when the easy pattern changes.

00:05:24.415 --> 00:05:26.256
Some conditions belong in practice.

00:05:26.256 --> 00:05:28.149
Others are reserved for later tests.

00:05:28.149 --> 00:05:33.315
Keeping those separate helps us judge whether a behavior extends beyond the examples it already saw.

00:05:33.315 --> 00:05:35.924
The simulation is a place to rehearse and compare.

00:05:35.924 --> 00:05:40.629
A physical compound introduces new conditions, new constraints, and human responsibilities.

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:44.619
Moving into that setting would require additional engineering and validation.

00:05:44.619 --> 00:05:49.990
That is why the vision includes people supervising the system and deciding where it is ready to be used.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:53.468
Learning supports the operation through a sequence of tested steps.

00:05:53.468 --> 00:05:57.560
The objective is dependable support for the people working inside the compound.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:00.298
Athena: Mira, the system will practice what we reward.

00:06:00.298 --> 00:06:04.880
How do we make sure a useful-looking number corresponds to a useful mission?

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.210
Dr. Mira Chen: Begin with the outcome.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.217
For Supply Run, finding the cargo is one milestone.

00:06:09.217 --> 00:06:13.843
The complete objective includes returning with the supplies and the whole team.

00:06:13.843 --> 00:06:16.849
That distinction changes how we interpret progress.

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:22.285
A policy can make one part of a task look impressive while leaving the larger job unfinished.

00:06:22.285 --> 00:06:27.952
In our future compound, the same reasoning applies to a delivery schedule or a maintenance queue.

00:06:27.952 --> 00:06:33.040
Speed is meaningful alongside completion, resource use, and the constraints people set.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.717
A reward is a design choice.

00:06:34.717 --> 00:06:39.574
It is our attempt to express an intention in a signal the learning process can use.

00:06:39.574 --> 00:06:42.523
We then inspect the behavior that signal produces.

00:06:42.523 --> 00:06:48.305
We also keep a separate evaluation: clear conditions, frozen policies, and outcomes we can compare.

00:06:48.305 --> 00:06:51.023
Training tells us what the system experienced.

00:06:51.023 --> 00:06:54.782
Evaluation tells us what the saved system did in a defined test.

00:06:54.782 --> 00:06:57.384
Shareholders should be able to ask for both.

00:06:57.384 --> 00:07:01.200
That is how evidence becomes part of the product we are building.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.901
Athena: Rowan, bring this back to the people building it.

00:07:03.901 --> 00:07:08.200
What does a productive day of reinforcement-learning work actually look like?

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:10.574
Rowan Vale: It starts with a specific question.

00:07:10.574 --> 00:07:18.034
We build or adjust a scenario, check that its rules work, and decide what evidence would answer the question.

00:07:18.034 --> 00:07:21.696
Then we run the experiment and inspect what happened.

00:07:21.696 --> 00:07:25.223
Sometimes the next useful change is an observation.

00:07:25.223 --> 00:07:30.038
Sometimes it is a reward, a demonstration, or a better test condition.

00:07:30.038 --> 00:07:33.429
The work is connecting those choices to behavior.

00:07:33.429 --> 00:07:42.109
Dane and his project partner direct that process: choosing the experiments, checking the results, and deciding what to improve.

00:07:42.109 --> 00:07:44.212
Unity makes the world visible.

00:07:44.212 --> 00:07:47.399
The training system makes practice repeatable.

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:50.044
Sentinel makes the record inspectable.

00:07:50.044 --> 00:07:57.640
Together they give us a way to move from an idea, to a test, to a decision about the next piece of engineering.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:07:59.827
Athena: Elias, give us the practical opportunity.

00:07:59.827 --> 00:08:04.520
What should someone watching this meeting recognize when they return to their own work?

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:08.275
Elias Ward: Look for a process where today's decision changes tomorrow's options.

00:08:08.275 --> 00:08:10.833
A warehouse team chooses how to sequence work.

00:08:10.833 --> 00:08:14.044
A service operation decides which task should happen next.

00:08:14.044 --> 00:08:17.908
A utility system balances changing demand against available resources.

00:08:17.908 --> 00:08:23.133
These are examples of decision problems where a learning approach might be worth investigating.

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:28.303
In our AEGIS world, those ideas come together in logistics, maintenance, energy, and training.

00:08:28.303 --> 00:08:34.181
The ambition is a compound whose supporting systems can be rehearsed together before people depend on them.

00:08:34.181 --> 00:08:41.148
Whether a learning method is useful will depend on the task, the quality of the simulation, the alternatives, and the evidence.

00:08:41.148 --> 00:08:44.032
Sometimes a simpler method will be the right choice.

00:08:44.032 --> 00:08:53.502
The business opportunity is the ability to make that comparison well: to test an idea, understand its tradeoffs, and spend effort where it produces a meaningful improvement.

00:08:53.502 --> 00:08:58.400
That is a practical engineering capability with applications beyond this fictional world.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.289
Athena: We have the world, the learning process, and a way to measure it.

00:09:02.289 --> 00:09:05.640
Mira, what turns that foundation into the next chapter?

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.581
Dr. Mira Chen: The next chapter is stronger coordination across the complete mission.

00:09:09.581 --> 00:09:18.870
We can target practice at the transitions between stages, improve demonstrations where they are useful, and test the saved team against the same defined conditions.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:24.274
As the behavior develops, we can broaden the conditions and compare it with a matched baseline.

00:09:24.274 --> 00:09:25.907
The questions stay concrete.

00:09:25.907 --> 00:09:27.933
Does the team complete the mission?

00:09:27.933 --> 00:09:29.228
Does it stay together?

00:09:29.228 --> 00:09:31.874
Does the behavior hold when the setup changes?

00:09:31.874 --> 00:09:35.590
We record the answers and use them to choose the next experiment.

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:44.316
The longer-term AEGIS vision builds outward from that discipline: richer simulations, more interconnected decisions, and carefully evaluated applications.

00:09:44.316 --> 00:09:47.637
A compelling world gives people a reason to pay attention.

00:09:47.637 --> 00:09:51.353
A repeatable research process gives us a reason to keep building.

00:09:51.353 --> 00:10:00.360
Our investment case today is that foundation: a working environment, specialized agents, and an expanding ability to test what intelligent teamwork can become.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.322
Athena: Thank you, Mira.

00:10:01.322 --> 00:10:03.006
Thank you, Elias and Rowan.

00:10:03.006 --> 00:10:12.208
Today you have seen the project at two scales: the real Unity world we have built, and the future AEGIS compound that gives the research a larger story.

00:10:12.208 --> 00:10:15.816
Reinforcement learning connects them through a simple idea.

00:10:15.816 --> 00:10:17.560
Decisions create experience.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.485
Experience can change a policy.

00:10:19.485 --> 00:10:24.898
The value comes from the behavior that follows, and from the evidence we use to judge it.

00:10:24.898 --> 00:10:27.664
As you leave, carry three questions with you.

00:10:27.664 --> 00:10:29.408
What can the system observe?

00:10:29.408 --> 00:10:30.792
What are we rewarding?

00:10:30.792 --> 00:10:33.799
And how will we know it learned something useful?

00:10:33.799 --> 00:10:40.174
Those questions belong in a simulation, in a training lab, and in the decisions we make about technology.

00:10:40.174 --> 00:10:41.617
This is AEGIS Dynamics.

00:10:41.617 --> 00:10:42.639
Learning to act.

00:10:42.639 --> 00:10:43.782
Learning together.

00:10:43.782 --> 00:10:46.308
Thank you for joining our annual meeting.

00:10:46.308 --> 00:10:49.075
No single system sees the entire battlefield.

00:10:49.075 --> 00:10:51.360
Intelligence must exist between them.
